<TopicPage slug="subjects/astronomy/advanced/galaxies-and-the-expanding-universe">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    A century ago, astronomers debated whether other galaxies existed. Today we know of
    <strong>trillions</strong>, arranged in filaments and voids — and their light tells us the
    universe is <strong>expanding</strong>, and was once hot, dense and small. The two decisive
    measurements — Hubble's recession law and the cosmic microwave background — turned the Big
    Bang from speculation into standard cosmology. This page assembles the full argument in
    the order the syllabus units build it: first the galaxies themselves (what they are, how
    they are classified, where they live), then the redshift law that made them move, then
    the age and size those numbers imply, then the microwave background that proved the hot
    beginning, and finally the dark matter and dark energy that dominate the cosmic budget —
    the two facts, one famous and one embarrassing, that the expert-tier lessons take further.
  </p>
  <p>
    The historical hinge deserves its own retelling, because it shows measurement
    outrunning philosophy. Through the 1910s, "spiral nebulae" were debated: were they
    gas clouds inside our star system, or separate "island universes"? The
    Shapley–Curtis debate (1920) argued both sides with no decisive data. Hubble
    settled it by finding <strong>Cepheid variable stars</strong> in Andromeda —
    pulsating stars whose period reveals their true brightness (the period–luminosity
    relation Henrietta Leavitt had calibrated), so their apparent dimness measures
    distance. Andromeda's Cepheids placed it ~900,000 light-years away (the modern
    figure is 2.5 million) — far outside even the largest estimate of the Milky Way.
    One calibrated star type, one revolution.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four discoveries that built modern cosmology" widget="equationStep" config={galaxiesCfg} />

  <h2>The galaxy census: types, groups, clusters, web</h2>
  <p>
    Hubble's <strong>tuning-fork classification</strong> still organises the census:
    <strong>ellipticals</strong> (E0 spherical to E7 flattened) are smooth swarms of old,
    red, low-mass stars with little gas and no current star formation — the universe's
    retirement homes; <strong>spirals</strong> (Sa–Sc, plus the <strong>barred</strong>
    SB branch — the Milky Way is a barred spiral) carry disks, spiral arms, gas and
    ongoing star formation; <strong>irregulars</strong> are gas-rich, shapeless dwarfs,
    often tidally mangled. The types are <em>histories</em>: mergers convert spirals to
    ellipticals, and the biggest ellipticals sit at cluster centres where mergers are
    frequent. Our own address: the <strong>Local Group</strong>, a modest cluster of
    ~80 galaxies dominated by the Milky Way, Andromeda (M31) and Triangulum, spanning
    ~10 million light-years — inside the <strong>Virgo Supercluster</strong>, itself a
    lobe of the <strong>Laniakea</strong> supercluster, threaded onto the cosmic web of
    filaments and voids that the Sloan Digital Sky Survey mapped galaxy by galaxy. The
    deepening irony of the census: Andromeda approaches us at 110 km/s (a local
    gravitational bound overriding the Hubble flow) and will merge with the Milky Way
    in ~4.5 billion years — a collision whose remnant is sometimes pre-named
    "Milkomeda".
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Active galactic nuclei</strong> (15.9–15.12 in the syllabus) are galaxies
    with engines: a <strong>supermassive black hole</strong> (millions to billions of
    solar masses) accreting matter at the centre, converting infalling gas to radiation
    with ~10% efficiency — several times better than fusion — and outshining the host
    galaxy's hundred billion stars from a region light-days across. <strong>Quasars</strong>,
    brightest of the class, are visible across most of the observable universe; radio
    galaxies shoot <strong>jets</strong> thousands of light-years from their poles; and
    our own Milky Way hosts a quiet version — Sagittarius A*, 4.3 million solar masses,
    imaged by the Event Horizon Telescope in 2022. The AGN census is also a time
    machine result: quasars cluster at redshift ~2, showing that black-hole feeding was
    a youth phenomenon — galaxies grew their engines early and mostly switched off.
  </p>

  <h2>Galaxies as ecosystems: how the census evolved</h2>
  <p>
    The modern view treats a galaxy as an ecosystem in balance: gas falls in, stars
    form, stellar winds and supernovae blow gas back out, and the central black hole's
    AGN phase heats what remains. The integrated star-formation history — measurable by
    summing every survey's infrared and ultraviolet light — peaked around redshift ~2
    ("cosmic noon", ~10 billion years ago) at perhaps ten times today's rate, and has
    declined ever since as the gas reservoirs deplete: the universe formed most of its
    stars early and is slowly turning off. Two structure facts complete the picture.
    First, the Milky Way's own inventory — a disk ~100,000 light-years across holding
    ~200 billion stars, a central bulge, a halo of globular clusters and dark matter —
    is mapped, outside the optical, by the <strong>21-cm line</strong> of cold hydrogen:
    radio emission that sails through dust, traced the spiral arms, and measured the
    rotation curve that began the dark-matter story. Second, galaxies are assembled
    <em>hierarchically</em>: the Milky Way is still consuming dwarf galaxies (the
    Sagittarius stream wraps our sky), and its halo preserves the chemical
    fingerprints of the dwarf galaxies it has already eaten — galactic archaeology,
    reading fossils in star orbits and metal abundances.
  </p>

  <h2>The expanding universe: redshift, Hubble's law, age and size</h2>
  <p>
    Expansion is measured as <strong>redshift</strong>: as space stretches, light's
    wavelength stretches with it, 1 + z = λ_observed/λ_emitted. (Doppler shifts from
    local motions also exist — Andromeda's blueshift — but cosmological redshift is
    the stretching of space itself during the photon's journey.) The pattern Hubble
    found in 1929 is linear: recession speed proportional to distance,
    <strong>v = H₀·d</strong>, with H₀ the <strong>Hubble constant</strong>. The full
    worked example, redshift to distance to time:
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="from redshift to distance to lookback time — the calculation pipeline every redshift survey runs" widget="equationStep" config={hubbleCfg} />

  <p>
    Three structural points. First, <strong>no centre</strong>: the expansion is the
    stretching of space everywhere, so every observer sees every other galaxy receding
    (the raisin-bread analogy — every raisin sees the others move apart; none is the
    middle). Second, <strong>lookback time</strong>: light travels at finite speed, so
    distant objects are seen as they were — a galaxy at z = 10 is seen ~13.3 billion
    years ago, and JWST's deep fields now image galaxies shining a few hundred million
    years after the Big Bang. Third, <strong>the age</strong>: running expansion
    backwards gives a Hubble time 1/H₀ ≈ 14 billion years, refined (accounting for the
    decelerating-then-accelerating history) to <strong>13.8 billion years</strong>
    (Planck 2018). The <strong>observable universe</strong> is bigger than 13.8 billion
    light-years <em>in radius</em> because space expanded while the light travelled:
    the current radius is about 46.5 billion light-years — expansion has outrun the
    light, which is also why we receive no signal from beyond that horizon.
  </p>

  <h2>The Big Bang's evidence: three pillars</h2>
  <p>
    The theory stands on three measured legs. <strong>First, the redshift law
    itself</strong> — recession proportional to distance, in every direction equally,
    exactly what a uniform expansion (not an explosion into pre-existing space)
    predicts. <strong>Second, primordial abundances</strong>: Big Bang nucleosynthesis
    computes the fusion of the first three minutes and predicts ~75% hydrogen, ~25%
    helium (by mass) with traces of deuterium and lithium — and the oldest, most
    pristine gas clouds match. <strong>Third, the cosmic microwave background</strong>:
    when the universe cooled below ~3,000 K (380,000 years in), electrons finally bound
    to nuclei and the fog of plasma became transparent, releasing the light we now see
    cooled and stretched to 2.725 K — a perfect blackbody glow filling all directions,
    predicted by Alpher and Herman in 1948 and found by Penzias and Wilson in 1965 as
    unremovable antenna noise (they checked the pigeon droppings first). The CMB's
    <strong>anisotropies</strong> — hot and cold patches of one part in 100,000, mapped
    by COBE (Nobel 2006), WMAP and Planck — are the money measurement: they are the
    density ripples that gravity grew into every galaxy and cluster (structure
    formation's initial conditions), and their statistical pattern pins down the cosmic
    composition directly.
  </p>

  <h2>The dark 95%: dark matter, dark energy, fate</h2>
  <p>
    The composition from those anisotropies: ordinary atoms ~5%, <strong>dark
    matter</strong> ~27%, <strong>dark energy</strong> ~68%. Dark matter's evidence is
    gravitational and independent: galaxy <strong>rotation curves stay flat</strong>
    far out (Vera Rubin's measurements — visible mass alone predicts speeds that fall
    off, observed ones do not); <strong>gravitational lensing</strong> maps mass where
    no light shines; and the <strong>Bullet Cluster</strong> shows the lensing mass
    passing through the collisional gas — dark matter behaving as predicted. It is
    non-luminous, non-interacting (so far), and — as the expert lesson develops —
    almost certainly not any known particle. Dark energy emerged in 1998, when two
    supernova teams measuring the expansion's history found distant supernovae
    <em>too dim</em>: the expansion is <strong>accelerating</strong> (Nobel 2011),
    attributed to a near-uniform energy of empty space (a cosmological constant, Λ).
    The fate question follows: with Λ dominant, expansion accelerates forever toward
    a cold, dilute "heat death"; galaxies beyond the Local Group will eventually cross
    the horizon and vanish from view. A <strong>Big Rip</strong> (dark energy
    strengthening over time) is disfavoured by current data; a recollapse is ruled out
    by the acceleration. The standard model's ledger is complete and bizarre: the
    stuff we are made of is the minority stake.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The equations under the story.</strong> General relativity (the expert-tier
    companion) yields the <strong>Friedmann equations</strong> governing the scale
    factor a(t): the universe's expansion rate is set by its density relative to the
    <strong>critical density</strong> ρ_c = 3H₀²/8πG ≈ 9 × 10⁻²⁷ kg/m³ — about five
    hydrogen atoms per cubic metre, the emptiest "material" in physics. Measured
    geometry (from CMB acoustic peaks) says the total is indistinguishable from
    critical: Ω ≈ 1, a <em>flat</em> universe — which inflation (the hypothesised
    exponential growth spurt at 10⁻³⁶ seconds, explaining flatness, horizon uniformity
    and the absence of magnetic monopoles) was designed to produce. Inflation remains
    the standard but unconfirmed extension; its smoke-and-fire test would be the
    primordial gravitational-wave imprint (B-modes) in the CMB's polarisation — claimed
    (BICEP2, 2014), retracted as galactic dust, still hunted.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The Hubble tension — cosmology's live controversy.</strong> The two best
    routes to H₀ disagree beyond their error bars: the cosmic distance ladder (Cepheids
    and supernovae, calibrated locally: ~73 km/s/Mpc) versus the CMB plus the standard
    model fitted backward (~67.4). Each team's systematic checks have survived
    scrutiny; the ~8% gap has not closed in a decade. Either a subtle measurement bias
    hides in one chain, or the standard model of cosmology (ΛCDM) is missing physics
    between the CMB era and today — new dark-sector particles, early dark energy,
    modified gravity. The 2020s' analogous puzzle: JWST finds galaxies apparently too
    massive, too mature at redshifts 10–14 for simple ΛCDM timelines — either early
    galaxy formation was startlingly efficient or the observations hide dust and
    crowding. Both tensions are the field's healthy open frontier: a standard model
    with two loose threads and a generation of instruments (JWST, Euclid, Vera Rubin
    Observatory, Simons Observatory) pulling on them.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Measurement craft worth knowing.</strong> The distance ladder is a chain of
    overlapping calibrators — parallax (the survey geometry from the intermediate
    tier) calibrates Cepheids, Cepheids calibrate Type Ia supernovae (standardisable
    candles whose physics is a white dwarf at the Chandrasekhar limit — the
    compact-objects lesson), supernovae calibrate the far field. Each link carries
    systematics; the field's history is a century of hunting them. The coming
    decade's new windows: <strong>21-cm cosmology</strong> (mapping the hydrogen of the
    "dark ages" before the first galaxies), <strong>gravitational-wave standard
    sirens</strong> (merging compact objects as distance markers needing no ladder at
    all), and weak lensing surveys that weigh the dark matter filaments directly. The
    redshift you computed in the widget remains the master coordinate of them all —
    everything else is engineering around it.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Hubble (1929); Penzias &amp; Wilson (1965); Planck Collaboration (2018); Riess et al. (1998) and Perlmutter et al. (1999) on acceleration; OpenStax, <em>Astronomy 2e</em> (Galaxies; The Big Bang).</p>
